For the kerndat.fnt.json file inside any given font's directory...

{
  "version": 1,
  "leading": 2,
  "charSpace": 2,
  "kernAmount": 2,
  "vertAlign": 1,
  "kerning": {
    " ": [3, 3],
    "!": [3, 3],
    "\"": [2, 2]
  }
}

leading         (0 to 255)
	The vertical distance between two lines from the top of the previous line to the top of the next line

charSpace       (0 to 255)
	The amount of space between each glyph when drawn onto the screen.

kernAmount      (0 to 255)
	The amount of kerning applied, in pixels, when kerning is available.
	Can not be more than charSpace.

vertAlign       (-128 to 127)
	Aligns the font vertically on-screen in pixels
	positive numbers push the font down while negative numbers bring it up

kerning         (optional)
	Each key is one character. Its value is [left_kern_mask, right_kern_mask]:

	left_kern_mask  (0 to 3)
		left-hand side kerning mask

	right_kern_mask (0 to 3)
		right-hand side kerning mask

If a font has no kerndat.fnt.json file, the leading is the height of the
font plus 1, and the font has no kerning.

scripts/convert_sounds_and_fonts.py converts the old kerndat.fnt files, which
have the line "font_name leading char_spacing kern_amount vert_alignment" and
then a line "hex_char left_kern_mask right_kern_mask" for each character, to
this format.

This information is parsed in libs/graphics/fontfile.cpp.
